data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1793970381208,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16 at 13:32 UTC; the command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water to the command module; the Lunar Module had descent and ascent stages.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Neil Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Buzz Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Michael Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 fulfilled the goal President John F. Kennedy set in May 1961 to land a man on the Moon before the decade ended.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"The program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 launchpad fire in January 1967 and built on Mercury and Gemini technologies.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Stepping onto the lunar surface, Armstrong declared: \\\"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples from the mission led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved in Washington, D.C., while Eagle's descent stage remains on the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth; Alan Shepard followed on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important, and none more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy's speech implies that going to the Moon would be a national effort, since everyone must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy spoke to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued outer space currently has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict, and its hazards are hostile to all mankind.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said space conquest deserves the best of all mankind and its opportunity for peaceful cooperation may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answered 'why the Moon?' by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy framed the Moon goal as a hard challenge that would organize and measure the nation's best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"In June 1961, Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing with Soviet Premier Khrushchev, who declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy again proposed a joint Moon expedition at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the idea was abandoned after his death.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Kennedy\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, the service module, and the lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module held the three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had descent and ascent stages, allowing landing on the Moon and return to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"This design allowed a single Saturn V rocket, then under development, to launch the spacecraft; Project Gemini developed key Apollo technologies.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"Apollo 7 in October 1968 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"Apollo 8 in December 1968 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 9 in March 1969 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; Apollo 10 in May 1969 conducted a lunar orbit 'dress rehearsal'.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, all was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Space Race\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"United States\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"3f548256649d0a4805239904dacacad5868118eb13ac5bf096e78d152ae2017c"}}

